  "step_3": "The decision reason requires evaluating which lottery is more right-skewed—i.e., which has a greater proportion of probability mass on unfavorable outcomes. The individual does not mention skewness or the relative weight of probabilities on favorable vs. unfavorable outcomes. Instead, they focus on the high probability of receiving any reward in A and the low probability of receiving anything in B, which aligns more with a risk-averse or safety-first heuristic. The individual’s justification is based on perceived certainty and risk aversion, not on the structural skewness of the distribution. While the individual does consider the probability of receiving zero (which relates to skewness), they do not frame this in terms of skewness or compare the two lotteries in terms of which is more right-skewed. The decision rule of the reason (prefer the more right-skewed lottery) is not reflected in the justification. Therefore, the decision reason was not used. The individual’s reasoning is consistent with risk aversion or a focus on the likelihood of receiving any payoff, not skewness-based evaluation.",
  "final_assessment_score": 0
